course: dictionary
lesson: 7351
title: Plausible: believable or sensible
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A plausible idea seems likely to be true, or sensible enough that people can believe it. You could say it another way: "that could really be true." Plausible is about how an idea sounds and fits the facts, not about whether it has been proved.
explain: Read this: Mia says, «My homework was eaten by the dog.» Her teacher thinks it is not plausible, because the homework was in her bag all night and the dog was asleep. A plausible excuse would fit the facts, like «The bus was late this morning.»
explain: In science, a plausible explanation fits the facts you have found. In maths, checking that an answer is plausible means asking if it is sensible: a child cannot be 300 cm tall.
explain: Plausible can also describe a person. A plausible person is good at making others believe things, even when those things are not true. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning is used.
fact: Plausible means seeming likely to be true or reasonable, so people can believe it.
fact: A plausible answer fits the facts and makes sense.
fact: «Plausible» can also describe a person who is good at making people believe things, sometimes when they are not true.
fact: In «"Plausible deniability" means an arrangement that lets leaders deny they knew about something», the leaders can say they did not know, and people might believe it.
fact: A good check is to ask: could this really be true, or does it fit the facts?
exercise: «The science club's plausible explanation fit every fact they had found.» What does plausible mean here? | Very long and boring | *Likely and believable | Lost and forgotten | Loud and colourful
exercise: Ben says he missed the bus because it was late. Mum thinks his excuse is plausible. What does that mean? | It is made of paper | It is funny and silly | *It seems believable and reasonable | It is impossible and wrong
exercise: Which answer to a maths problem is NOT plausible? | A child is 140 cm tall | A child is 120 cm tall | *A child is 300 cm tall
test: «The con artist was so plausible that everyone trusted him.» What does plausible mean here? | Very quiet and shy | Large in size | Easy to see through | *Good at making people believe things, even untrue ones
test: Which word is closest in meaning to plausible? | lazy | tiny | *believable | strange
exam: Ruby says, «Your story is not plausible, because the dragon could not fit in the classroom.» What does she mean? | Her story is only about dragons | *Her story is hard to believe, so it does not seem likely to be true | Her story has too many words | Her story is very long
